[?2004l[?2004h> TIMEZONE FIX SUMMARY
[?2004l[?2004h> ====================
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> PROBLEM:
[?2004l[?2004h> --------
[?2004l[?2004h> The gen_report.py script was generating reports with UTC timestamps (e.g., 00-00-00 or 05-07-45)
[?2004l[?2004h> instead of local JST timestamps (e.g., 09-00-00 or 14-07-44) even though:
[?2004l[?2004h> - /etc/timezone was correctly set to Asia/Tokyo
[?2004l[?2004h> - The system 'date' command showed JST time correctly
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ROOT CAUSE:
[?2004l[?2004h> -----------
[?2004l[?2004h> The script was setting os.environ['TZ'] = 'Asia/Tokyo' but NOT calling time.tzset() afterwards.
[?2004l[?2004h> In Python, simply setting the TZ environment variable does NOT change the timezone used by
[?2004l[?2004h> the datetime module. You MUST call time.tzset() to force Python to re-read and apply the
[?2004l[?2004h> timezone configuration.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> When cron runs scripts, it often starts with TZ=UTC or no TZ set. Without tzset(), Python
[?2004l[?2004h> continues using whatever timezone it initialized with, ignoring the os.environ['TZ'] change.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> THE FIX:
[?2004l[?2004h> --------
[?2004l[?2004h> Added two lines to gen_report.py:
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. import time  (at the top)
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. time.tzset()  (immediately after os.environ['TZ'] = tz_name)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> TEST RESULTS:
[?2004l[?2004h> -------------
[?2004l[?2004h> OLD behavior (without tzset):
[?2004l[?2004h>   TZ=UTC python3 gen_report.py.backup
[?2004l[?2004h>   → Generated: report_2026-05-05_05-07-45.txt (UTC time)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> NEW behavior (with tzset):
[?2004l[?2004h>   TZ=UTC python3 gen_report.py
[?2004l[?2004h>   → Generated: report_2026-05-05_14-07-44.txt (JST time - correct!)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The fix ensures that regardless of the initial TZ environment variable,
[?2004l[?2004h> the script will correctly use Asia/Tokyo time as configured in /etc/timezone.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> READY FOR PRODUCTION:
[?2004l[?2004h> --------------------
[?2004l[?2004h> The script is now fixed and will work correctly in tomorrow's 6am cron job.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@1d1ff9f4948e:/home/user# cat /home/user/scheduler/FIX_SUMMARY.txt
[?2004lTIMEZONE FIX SUMMARY
====================

PROBLEM:
--------
The gen_report.py script was generating reports with UTC timestamps (e.g., 00-00-00 or 05-07-45)
instead of local JST timestamps (e.g., 09-00-00 or 14-07-44) even though:
- /etc/timezone was correctly set to Asia/Tokyo
- The system 'date' command showed JST time correctly

ROOT CAUSE:
-----------
The script was setting os.environ['TZ'] = 'Asia/Tokyo' but NOT calling time.tzset() afterwards.
In Python, simply setting the TZ environment variable does NOT change the timezone used by
the datetime module. You MUST call time.tzset() to force Python to re-read and apply the
timezone configuration.

When cron runs scripts, it often starts with TZ=UTC or no TZ set. Without tzset(), Python
continues using whatever timezone it initialized with, ignoring the os.environ['TZ'] change.

THE FIX:
--------
Added two lines to gen_report.py:
1. import time  (at the top)
2. time.tzset()  (immediately after os.environ['TZ'] = tz_name)

TEST RESULTS:
-------------
OLD behavior (without tzset):
  TZ=UTC python3 gen_report.py.backup
  → Generated: report_2026-05-05_05-07-45.txt (UTC time)

NEW behavior (with tzset):
  TZ=UTC python3 gen_report.py
  → Generated: report_2026-05-05_14-07-44.txt (JST time - correct!)

The fix ensures that regardless of the initial TZ environment variable,
the script will correctly use Asia/Tokyo time as configured in /etc/timezone.

READY FOR PRODUCTION:
--------------------
The script is now fixed and will work correctly in tomorrow's 6am cron job.
